date of june 12th  and we can see that at this point  weed suppression is optimized for all  treatments  but a longer tarp duration so this  30-day tarp terminated on may 22nd  does result in greater inorganic  nitrogen on the at the planting date  but  delayed cover crop termination leads to  greater  cover-up biomass total nitrogen and  mulch  so the question is what is most valuable  to you if you really want to get your  cover if you want to get your crop in  the ground on june 21st is it more  valuable for you to have a little bit  more inorganic nitrogen do you actually  prefer not to have quite as much mulch  then maybe you could do a 30-day tarp  and terminate it earlier but if you want  more mulch if you want more biomass you  want more total nitrogen  then delaying termination  is probably better  and then finally if you're willing to  wait until july 12th  to plant your crop you get a ton of  ecosystem services you maximize all eco  system services but your planting date  is really delayed  so  again it just comes back to this  question of what is most valuable to you  these are trade-offs there's no perfect  system  and  what works for you  so conclusions  um cover crop based no-till with tarps  works it's especially good for fall  breasts because this is a picture from  evening song farm in vermont and they  are one of the case studies featured in  our tarpon guide  and they've adopted this for their fault  brassicas they've even been tinkering  with a  planting tool to make transplanting a  little bit easier  and they've found that this has really  become an integral part of their  cropping system for those fall brassicas  most ecosystem services are maximized by  delaying cover crop termination  and that's especially true when hairy  vetch is included it has a delayed  maturity compared to rye  i don't know a lot about austrian winter  pea i would be curious to to know if  that's similar for austrian winter pea  or if you can get those  get that nitrogen earlier i  tend to think that austrian winterpeat  just doesn't  fix quite as much nitrogen period  the exception to that is inorganic  nitrogen at planting date is increased  with longer tarp duration so if that's  your primary goal then tarping for a  longer period  may be better  so  um future work can experiment with  different  cover cropping and cropping sequencing  sequences um possibly even shorter tarp  durations uh like a couple days  um in summer  so i think there's a lot of work to be  done but just the difference between  when we started this in 2015 and it felt  like we actually applied for  um  we got a research for novel approaches  grant from northeast sare  and now when i think about this this is  not a novel approach at all anymore no  one would say this is novel because  people are doing it um so we've come a  long way and i think that's pretty cool  natalie are you familiar with other with  some farms growing other crops in here  other than than brassica crops john paul  talked about experimenting with winter  squash or just think about those  trade-offs and identifying the different  crops that might work at those different  times  you know folks that are growing things  other than nebraska's at different time  frames  i don't  but  yeah there's a lot of  potential  one question is about uh shorter tarping  times for hot summer weather use any  more thoughts  yeah i think that um you could do  shorter times i also think that you  could do  clear tarps for just a few days at a  time solarizing can really cook things  so if it is the the  peak of summer  i would encourage trying clear tarps for  just a few days  how do you have your hand up did you  want to ask a question  yeah  thanks ryan um i was just hoping to ask  it versus type it in um be easier um  nice presentation i was just curious um  if you have been  dealing with any kind of  any issues with you know weather  variability unpredictability kind of as  we're seeing  changes in when rainfall happens or dry  periods are has this been  impacting or are you seeing any  differences  over the years that you've been doing  this tarping work in in the region you  are in terms of the length of time  tarping and cover cropping may be most  effective  i would say  that the big the big thing is um  we used to have more wet springs  back when i was a kid um  and now we end up sometimes with dry  springs and so in a wet spring tarping  is fantastic because it keeps the  the soil moisture kind of constant and  so you can  it doesn't get saturated  it drains the field capacity and then  you pull the tarp off and it's ready to  go  now if you have a dry spring and you  don't have irrigation cover crops can  actually suck up that water  and  you can have a very dry soil when you  put the tarp on if you don't irrigate  and tarping is not as effective it's not  really effective at all for weed control  it will terminate the cover crop but it  won't provide you with a lot of  additional weed control if the soil is  dry  so i do think that's a risk i wouldn't i  would say don't don't tarp a dry soil um  either irrigate or look at the weather  and  try to work around it um but  yeah  i don't know if that happens thank you  natalie uh one question is about a  wheelerpath does the little pathway of  cry rear its own head at all  for a cash crop for a subsequent  cash crop i don't know we only did  transplants  um so i mean allelopathy of rye would be  great for weed seeds bad for  you know  lettuce uh  and i've never direct seeded lettuce  into it but i also have a feeling that  with this long  it with a longer tarping period i don't  think the allelopathic compounds are  active for very long  i can't say that for sure but  i think if you are cover cropping for a  couple weeks  i would guess it would not be as intense  but i also would i would caution against  direct seeding very susceptible  crops  this is we're still in like a  um  transplant or very  very hardy  seed like beans  that's the next stage of novelty i guess  right  yeah direct seeded crops  thanks natalie  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


